<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?> 

<section xml:id="section_11_6" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Least squares regression</title>

<subsection> 
 <title>Predicting height from footprints</title>
<p>Can a footprint taken at the scene of a crime help to predict the height of the criminal? In other words, is there an association between height and foot length? To investigate this question, a sample of 20 statistics students measured their height (in inches) and their foot length (in centimeters).</p>

<activity> 
 <!--  Question 1  -->
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                What are the observational units in this study?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The observational units are the 20 statistics students.
          </p>
        </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Identify the two variables recorded for each observational unit. Which is the explanatory variable, and which is the response? Also classify these variables as categorical or quantitative.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The explanatory variable is foot length, quantitative; the response variable is height,
quantitative.
          </p>
        </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Is this an observational study or a randomized experiment?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            This is an observational study.
          </p>
        </solution>
    </task>
</activity>

<activity>
  <!--  Question 2 -->
  <statement>
      <p>
          The sample data for the 20 students appear in the <url href="https://static-content.zybooks.com/applets/2021/regshuffle/regshuffle.htm?hideExtras=2">Corr/Regression applet</url>
      </p>
      <p>
          Open the applet and look at the scatterplot for the data already there. Describe the association between the variables as revealed in the scatterplot. (Hint: Remember to comment on direction, strength, and form of association as well as unusual observations.)
      </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      The scatterplot shows a moderately strong positive linear association between foot length and
height.
    </p>
  </solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--  Question 3 -->
<statement>
<p>Would you say that a straight line could summarize the relationship between height and foot length reasonably well?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Yes.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity> 
 <!--  Question 4  -->
   <introduction>
    <p>
        Check the <term>Show Movable Line</term> box to add a blue line to the scatterplot. If you place your mouse over one of the green squares at the ends of the line and drag, you can change the slope of the line. You can also use the mouse to move the green dot up and down vertically to change the intercept of the line.
    </p>
   </introduction>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Move the line until you believe your line "best" summarizes the relationship between height and foot length for these data. Write down the resulting equation for your line (using traditional statistical notation).
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Answers will vary, eg., Movable line was: predicted height = 26.27 + 1.46 (footlength)
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Why do you believe that your line is "best"?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Answers may vary.  One potential: Because there weren't many points far away from the line.
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Did all students in your class obtain the same line/equation?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        No.
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            How can we decide whether your line provides a better fit to the data than other students' lines? Suggest a criterion for deciding which line "best" summarizes the relationship.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Answers may vary.  One potential: The line that is closest to all the points.
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
</activity>

<p>One way to draw the best fit line is to minimize the distance of the points to the line (these distances are called <em>residuals</em>).</p>

<activity>
 <!--  Question 5 -->
<statement>
<p>Would points above the line have a positive or negative residual, or is it impossible to tell? What about points below the line?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Above: positive residual
Below: negative residual
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<remark> 
 <!--  Key Idea  -->
<p>A <term>residual</term> is the difference between an observed response and the corresponding prediction made by the least squares regression line (residual = observed − predicted). Thus, negative residuals occur when points are below the best fit line and positive residuals occur when points are above the best fit line.</p>
</remark>

<activity> 
 <!--  Question 6  -->
   <introduction>
    <p>
        Check the <term>Show Residuals</term> box to visually represent these residuals for your line on the scatterplot. The applet also reports the sum of the values of the residuals (<term>SAE</term>). SAE stands for "sum of the absolute errors." The acronym indicates that we need to make residuals positive before we add them up and that sometimes people call residuals "errors." 
    </p>
   </introduction>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Record the SAE value for your line. What is the best (lowest) SAE in the class?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Answers will vary, e.g., SAE = 63.98
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
</activity>

<activity> 
 <!--  Question 7  -->
   <introduction>
    <p>
        It turns out that a more common criterion for determining the “best” line is to instead look at the sum of the squared residuals (<term>SSE</term>). This approach is similar to simply adding up the residuals but is even more strict in not letting individual residuals get too large. Check the <term>Show Squared Residuals</term> box to visually represent the squared residual for each observation. Note that we can visually represent the squared residual as the area of a square where each side of the square has length equal to the residual.
    </p>
   </introduction>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            What is the SSE (sum of squared residuals) for your line? What is the best (lowest) SSE in the class?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Answers will vary, e.g., SSE = 275.22
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Now continue to adjust your line until you think you have minimized the sum of the squared residuals. Report your new equation and your new SSE value. What is the best SSE in the class?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Answers will vary, e.g., New equation: predicted height = 32.1 + 1.15 (footlength); new
SSE = 235.01
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
</activity>

<remark> 
 <!--  Key Idea  -->
<p>The least squares regression line minimizes the sum of squared residuals.</p>
</remark>

<activity> 
 <!--  Question 8  -->
   <introduction>
    <p>
        Now check the <term>Show Regression Line</term> box to determine and display the equation for the line that actually does minimize (as can be shown using calculus) the sum of the squared residuals.
    </p>
   </introduction>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Record the equation of the least squares regression line by indicating the appropriate slope and intercept of the line. Note that we've used variable names in the equation, not generic x and y. And put a caret ("hat") over the y variable name to emphasize that the line gives predicted values of the y (response) variable.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Least squares regression line is: predicted height = 38.30 + 1.03 (footlength)
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Did everyone in your class obtain the same equation?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Yes.
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Is the slope positive or negative? Explain how the sign of the slope tells you about whether your data display a positive or a negative association.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The slope is positive so that tells us there is a positive association between the two
variables (as one increases the other one does as well).
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
</activity>

<remark> 
 <!--  Notation  -->
   <p>
        The equation of the best fit line is written as <m>\hat{y} = a + bx</m>, where
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <m>a</m> is the y-intercept
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <m>b</m> is the slope
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <m>x</m> is a value of the explanatory variable
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <m>\hat{y}</m> is the predicted value for the response variable
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
   </p>
</remark>

<remark> 
 <!--  Key Idea  -->
<p>For a given dataset, the signs (positive or negative) for the correlation coefficient and the slope of the regression line must be the same.</p>
</remark>

<activity> 
 <!--  Question 9  -->
   <introduction>
    <p>
        Let's investigate what the slope means in the context of height and foot length.
    </p>
   </introduction>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Use the least squares regression line to predict the height of someone whose foot length is 28 cm. (Simply plug in the value of 28 cm for foot length in the equation of the line.)
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        67.14
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Use the least squares regression line to predict the height of someone whose foot length is 29 cm.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        68.17
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            By how much do your predictions in (a) and (b) differ? Does this number look familiar? Explain.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        They differ by 1.03, which is the slope.
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Interpret the slope in context:
The slope of the regression line predicting height based on foot length is ______, meaning that for every additional ______ cm increase in foot length, the predicted height increases by ______ inches.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The slope of the regression line predicting height based on foot length is 1.03, meaning
that for every additional one cm increase in foot length, the predicted height increases
by 1.03 inches.
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
</activity>

<remark> 
 <!--  Key Idea  -->
<p>The <term>slope</term> coefficient of a least squares regression model is interpreted as the predicted change in the response (<m>y</m>) variable for a one-unit change in the explanatory (<m>x</m>) variable.
</p>
</remark>

<activity> 
 <!--  Question 10  -->
   <introduction>
    <p>
        Let's investigate the meaning of the y-intercept in the context of height and foot length.
    </p>
   </introduction>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Use the least squares regression line to predict the height of someone who has a foot length of 0 cm.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        38.30 inches
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Your answer to (a) should look familiar. What is this value?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        This is the y-intercept.
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Explain how your prediction of the height of someone whose foot length is 0 cm is an example of extrapolation.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        There are no individuals with a foot length of 0 in the data. The lowest foot length is 22
cm., and most range from the mid-20s to the mid-30s.
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
</activity>

<remark> 
 <!--  Key Idea  -->
<p>The <term>y-intercept</term> of a regression line is interpreted as the predicted value of the response variable when the explanatory variable has a value of zero (though be wary of extrapolation (see the next Key Idea) in interpreting the intercept or other values outside the original data range).
</p>
</remark>

<remark> 
 <!--  Key Idea  -->
<p>Predicting values for the response variable for values of the explanatory variable that are outside of the range of the original data is known as <term>extrapolation</term> and can give very misleading predictions.</p>
</remark>

<activity> 
 <!--  Question 11  -->
   <introduction>
    <p>
        Earlier, we explored the notion of a residual as the distance of a point to a line.
    </p>
   </introduction>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Using the equation for the best fit line that you reported in question 8, find the residual for the person whose foot length is 32 centimeters and height is 74 inches. Note: Find this value by taking the actual height and subtracting the person's predicted height from the equation.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        7 4 - 71.26 = 2.74
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Is this person's dot on the scatterplot above or below the line? How does the residual tell you this?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Above the line, because the residual is positive.
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
</activity>

<p>Residuals are helpful for identifying unusual observations. But not all observations of interest have large residuals.
</p>

<activity> 
 <!--  Question 12  -->
   <introduction>
    <p>
        Uncheck the <term>Show Movable Line</term> box to remove it from the display and check the <term>Move observations</term> box.
    </p>
   </introduction>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Now click on one student's point in the scatterplot and drag the point up and down (changing the height, without changing the foot length, the original regression line will remain in grey). Does the regression line change much as you change this student's height?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Answers will vary, the regression line changes a little, but not much.
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Press Revert and repeat (a) using a student with a very small x (foot size) value and then a point with an x value near the middle and then a point with a very large x value. Which of these seem(s) to have the most influence on the least squares regression line? Explain.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Moving the very small foot size or the very large one seems to have the most influence
on the line. The slope changes the most when the small value is moved up, or the large
value is moved down.
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
</activity>

<remark> 
 <!--  Key Idea  -->
<p>An observation or set of observations is considered <term>influential</term> if removing the observation from the dataset substantially changes the values of the correlation coefficient and/or the least squares regression equation. Typically, observations that have extreme explanatory variable values (far below or far above <m>\bar{x}</m>) are potentially influential. They may not have large residuals, having pulled the line close to them.
</p>
</remark>

<p>Residuals also help us measure how accurate our predictions are from using the regression line. In particular, we can compare the “prediction errors” from the regression line to the prediction errors if we made no use of the explanatory variable.</p>

<activity> 
 <!--  Question 13  -->
   <introduction>
    <p>
        Press the <term>Revert</term> button to reload the original dataset. Recheck the <term>Show Movable Line</term> box to redisplay the blue line. Notice that this line is flat at the mean of the <m>y</m> (height) values.
    </p>
   </introduction>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Check the Show Squared Residuals box (under the Movable Line information) to determine the SSE if we were to use the average height as the predicted value for every x (foot size). Record this value.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        SSE =475.75
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            What is the slope of this line?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        0
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            If the slope of the best fit line is zero, our data shows ______ (positive/negative/no) association between the explanatory and response variables.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        No
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--  Question 14 -->
<statement>
<p>Now compare this to the SSE value from the regression line. Which is smaller? Why does that make sense?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    SSE is smaller from the regression line. This makes sense because the regression line is the best
fitting line and does so by minimizing the sum of the squared errors.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

</subsection>

<subsection> 
 <title>Coefficient of Determination </title>

<remark> 
 <!--  Question 15  -->
<p>A quantity related to the correlation coefficient is called the coefficient of determination, or R-squared (R²).
To measure how much the regression line decreases the "unexplained variability" in the response variable, we can calculate the percentage reduction in SSE:
<me>
    R^2 = \frac{\operatorname{SSE}(\bar{y}) - \operatorname{SSE}(\text{regression line})}{\operatorname{SSE}(\bar{y})}
</me>
</p>
<p>The <term>coefficient of determination</term> (<m>R^2</m>) is the percentage of total observed variation in the response variable (height) that is accounted for by changes (variability) in the explanatory variable (foot length).
</p>
</remark>

<activity>
 <!--  Question 15a -->
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Find <m>R^2</m> and report it.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <m>R^2 = 0.506 = 50.6\%</m>
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Complete the following statement: The coefficient of determination is ______ ; this means that ______ % of the variation in people's ______ is attributable to changes in their ______.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        50.6%, this means that 50.6% of the variation in people's height is attributed to changes
in their foot length.
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
   <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Find the value of the correlation coefficient using the applet and confirm that when you square the correlation coefficient you get the same number as the applet reports for the coefficient of determination.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        r = 0.711, and <m>r^2 = (0.7112^2) \approx 0.506</m>, the coefficient of determination.
      </p>
    </solution>
   </task>
</activity>

<p>If we think of <m>\hat{y} = \bar{y}</m> as the "worst" line (no association with <m>x</m>), then we are seeing how much better the actual line is than this worst line. The best possible line would have zeros for all of the residuals and would have an <m>R^2</m> of 100%.</p>

</subsection>

<subsection> 
 <title>Exploring further</title>
<activity>
 <!--  Question 16 -->
<statement>
<p>What other variables could explain variation in height?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Sex could explain variation in height.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<p>The figure below illustrates which people in the sample are men and which are women and
then plots the least squares regression line separately for men and women.</p>


<figure xml:id="fig-11-3">
    <image source="11-3/height-vs-foot.png">
        <shortdescription>A scatter plot with color-coded dots and regression lines that closely fit the respective colors. The male line is higher than the female line. The two lines have similar slopes.
        </shortdescription>
        </image>
</figure>

<activity>
 <!--  Question 17 -->
<statement>
<p>How do the female foot lengths tend to compare to the male foot lengths? How do the female heights tend to compare to the male height?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Female foot lengths and heights tend to be smaller than male foot lengths and heights.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--  Question 18 -->
<statement>
<p>What do you notice about the slopes and intercepts for the two lines? How do you interpret these differences in this context?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    The slopes are fairly similar with the male’s slope being a bit steeper; the y-intercept for the
females is larger than for the males.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<p>The least squares regression line for men is <m>\text{predicted height} = 44.03 + 0.8561(\text{foot length})</m>,
and the least squares regression line for women is <m>\text{predicted height} = 45.90 + 0.7152(\text{foot length})</m> .</p>
<activity>
 <!--  Question 19 -->
<statement>
<p>What do you think the R² will be just for the men? Just for the women? How do you think these values will compare to the overall R² of 0.506? Why?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Answers will vary, there is still quite a bit of variability about both the male's and female's lines,
so the <m>R^2</m> for men and the <m>R^2</m> for women will not change much from the overall <m>R^2</m>
.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<p>In this case, the <m>R^2</m> for the female-only data is 0.116 and for the male-only data is 0.267. Both groups have lower <m>R^2</m> values than the overall <m>R^2</m>. <m>R^2</m> measures how close the observations are to their respective lines. When we examine only the female data, the residuals are large relative to the smaller variation in female heights. Whereas the combined line (ignoring sex) has similar residuals, they are smaller compared to the larger total variation in heights.</p>
</subsection>

</section>